In C, `tv_sec` is `time_t` and `tv_nsec` is `long`. The subtraction happens in integer arithmetic, and the result is then converted to `double`. The seconds difference is `time_t` (i64 on 64-bit Linux) and is converted to `double` when it is added to the fractional part. The nanosecond difference is `long`, divided by the double `1e9`.

I use `libc::timespec` and take raw pointers, as in the C signature. Integer subtraction in C would be undefined behavior on overflow, so I use `wrapping_sub` to avoid a panic in debug builds.

